# SYNOPSIS

```text
page=NAME sect=N summary="..."
    names=... src=... keys=...

# HEADING

Paragraph text, with `literal`, <param>, name(N) and {label|target}.

@node=id title="Title"
```

# DESCRIPTION

A page of the manual is one UTF-8 file in guide, the format this page describes.
It holds a header, then a body of lines.
Each body line's kind is decided by how it begins, so a reader needs no more than the current line and whether it is inside a fence.
The page reads well with `cat`; man(1) refills it to the terminal's width.
Every reader of the format parses it with `lib/vx-guide`, and nothing else does.

The parser is strict.
What it does not know it refuses, with the line, and it never guesses: a typo in a key, a fence kind or a link target is an error, not a page shown wrongly.

A page may start with the line `@guide=1`.
It names the version of the format, and a page without it is version 1, permanently.
A later version is a new value; the parser refuses one it does not know.

@node=header title="The header"

The header is ndb records (see ndb(6)), ended by the first blank line.
It is one record, and its first tuple is `page=`.
Its lines after the first are indented, as any ndb record's may be.
Its keys are:

: `page`
  The page's name, in lower case: a letter or digit, then letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `+` or `-`.
  It is also the file's name, `/lib/man/<sect>/<page>`.
: `sect`
  The section, `1` to `8`.
: `summary`
  One line for NAME and the index, with no full stop.
: `names`
  Every name the page documents, separated by commas.
  It defaults to the page's name.
  Each is an index entry and a link target.
: `src`
  The repository paths SOURCE lists, separated by commas.
: `lang`
  `c`, `lua` or `rc`: the language of a section 2 page's interface.
: `level`
  The ABI level that introduced a section 2 page's names.
: `host`
  A flag: the page's program runs on the development host, not on VectraOS.
: `keys`
  More words for lookman(1), separated by commas.

`page`, `sect` and `summary` are required.
Any other key is an error.
The author never writes NAME or SOURCE: man(1) makes them from the header, and a heading of either is refused.

@node=blocks title="Blocks"

A body line is one of these, by how it starts:

| Start | Is |
| (blank) | the end of a paragraph, list item or definition |
| `# ` | a section heading |
| `## ` | a subsection heading |
| `- ` | a list item |
| `: ` | a definition's term, described by the indented lines after |
| two spaces | a continuation of the list item or definition above |
| `|` | a table row; the first row of a run is the header |
| ```` ``` ```` | a fence |
| `@` | a directive |
| anything else | paragraph text, its lines joined and refilled |

Headings are in capitals, in the order Plan 9's pages keep: SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, EXAMPLES, FILES, SEE ALSO, DIAGNOSTICS, BUGS.
A heading of the author's own is in capitals too.
A line starting `#` but not with `#` or `##` and a space is an error.

There is no nesting.
A list item holds no list and a definition no fence; a page that wants more structure wants a node.
A continuation line with nothing above to continue is an error.

A table row's cells are separated by `|`; a `|` inside a literal belongs to its cell, and the row's last `|` may be left off.
A run of rows is one table, its columns as wide as their widest cell.

@node=fences title="Fences"

A line of three backticks, followed by a kind or by nothing, opens a fence; a line of three backticks alone closes it.
What lies between is shown exactly as written, never refilled, and must fit 80 columns.
A fence that is never closed is an error.
The kinds are:

: `usage`
  A command's synopsis in Plan 9's notation: the command and its flags literal, every other word a parameter, `[ ]` optional and `...` repeated.
  It is the program's usage message: the build makes the message from it.
: `c`
  C declarations in a section 2 SYNOPSIS, checked against the installed header; elsewhere, example code.
: `rc`
  A transcript or a script.
  Lines starting with `%` and a space are typed; the others are output.
: `ndb`
  ndb records.
: `lua`
  Lua.
: `text`
  Anything: a diagram, a table of bytes.
  A fence with no kind is the same.

Any other kind is an error.

@node=inline title="Text inside a line"

Four things are special in paragraphs, list items, definitions, headings and table cells, and nothing else is:

: `` `cat -u` ``
  A literal: what is typed, a file's name, code.
  A literal opened by <n> backticks closes at the next run of exactly <n>, so one containing a backtick is opened by more.
  With two or more, one space just inside each end is dropped, so a literal may start with a backtick.
  An unclosed literal is an error.
: `<file>`
  A parameter: `<`, a letter, then letters, digits, `.`, `_` or `-`, then `>`.
: `rc(1)`
  A link to a page: a name followed by a section number in parentheses.
: `{label|target}`, `{target}`
  A link with words of its own, or to a node, or out of the manual.
  man(1) shows it as `label (target)`, or as the target alone.

A link's target is `name(N)`, `name(N)#node`, `#node` for a node of the same page, or a URL whose scheme is `https:`, `gemini:` or `gopher:`.
Any other target, and a `{` with no `}`, is an error.
A literal `{`, a `<word>` that is not a parameter, or a `name(2)` that is not a link goes inside backticks.
There is no emphasis.

@node=nodes title="Nodes"

A page is one node unless it says otherwise.
A line `@node=<id>` starts a node, its id lower-case letters, digits and `-`, unique in the page; it may carry `title=`, which a contents list shows, and `keys=`, more words for lookman(1).
Text before the first node is the main node, which every page has, and which holds SYNOPSIS and DESCRIPTION.

Reading order is the file's order, so a page split into nodes still prints, greps and pipes as one text: `man rc` prints every node, and `man rc quoting` prints one.
Nodes are for long pages; a short page has none.
A page has at most 64.

`@node` is the only directive.
Any other is an error, and `@guide=` is allowed only on the first line.

# EXAMPLES

A page, whole:

```text
page=cat sect=1 summary="concatenate files"
    src=cmd/cat.c

# SYNOPSIS

```

followed by a `usage` fence holding `cat [-u] [file ...]`, then:

```text
# DESCRIPTION

`cat` reads each <file> in order and writes it to standard
output. With no <file> it reads standard input.

: `-u`
  Write each read as it arrives.

# SEE ALSO

tail(1), read(2)
```

# FILES

: `/lib/man/<sect>/<page>`
  The installed pages.
: `man/<sect>/<page>`
  The pages in the repository, mirroring the installed tree.

# SEE ALSO

man(1), ndb(6); the design is `docs/12-manual.md` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

The parser reports the first error it meets with its line, such as `a fence kind guide does not know`, and stops.

# BUGS

A fence cannot show a line of three backticks alone, since that line closes it; the example above is split in two for that reason.
